1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,500
And he couldn't think of one.

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:04,040
Right? So he couldn't think of any exception

3
00:00:04,440 --> 00:00:08,440
of any cognitive skill which actually negatively correlates

4
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:09,640
with other cognitive skills.

5
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:13,560
Okay. So when you think about something like SAT verbal

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,020
versus math scores, you've got a friend who says I'm great

7
00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:17,200
at verbal but terrible at math.

8
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:20,780
Is that person actually normally terrible at math?

9
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:24,620
No. It's more like they're at like 95th percentile of verbal

10
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:26,380
and only 65th percentile of math.

11
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:28,000
And that in their minds is terrible.

12
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,280
Even though they're well above average on both things.

13
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:34,260
Okay. All right.

14
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:38,120
So that is a problem.

15
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:42,840
So this is true but again, it doesn't really actually show

16
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:43,880
that the tests don't mean anything

17
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:44,700
or that you shouldn't use them.

18
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:46,760
It just means well, yeah, so there isn't a perfect correlation

19
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:49,880
but still cognitive abilities do generally go together.

20
00:00:50,420 --> 00:00:53,320
And even things that are weird like athletic ability,

21
00:00:53,860 --> 00:00:56,200
the standard result is there's a correlation of.1

22
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:58,000
between IQ and athletic ability.

23
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:01,080
.1 is really small but it's something.

24
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:06,140
Right? And I guess the idea is that being smart allows people

25
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:09,740
to go and play sports more intelligently.

26
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:12,160
A little bit.

27
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:15,760
Yeah. So I have heard people know football when I say,

28
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:17,200
well, like quarterbacks generally test

29
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:18,900
as the highest IQ members of the team.

30
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:20,000
All right.

31
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:21,100
So things like that.

32
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:22,420
All right.

33
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:23,860
Now third complaint.

34
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:26,500
And this one is 100% totally true.

35
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:27,540
Imperfection.

36
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:28,920
The tests aren't perfect.

37
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:30,140
They totally are.

38
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:31,120
All right.

39
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:33,360
Now there's several varieties of complaints

40
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:34,280
about the imperfection.

41
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:36,900
So one of them is the same person gets very different scores

42
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:38,320
at different times and that's true.

43
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:41,600
It's rare that the test scores are really different

44
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:42,420
but it does happen.

45
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:46,360
Usually it's things like the person was sick one time

46
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:47,780
and so that can explain it.

47
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:51,880
Let's say Robin Hanson had a son who was told

48
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:55,620
that he would get a break during one of these tests

49
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:57,640
and then they changed it on him and he didn't get a break

50
00:01:57,640 --> 00:02:00,300
and he got really upset and then his performance

51
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:01,920
on the second part went way down.

52
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:04,020
And then they said, all right, fine, let's just retest him

53
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:05,300
and this time we don't tell him that.

54
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:07,440
This time we tell him there will be no break.

55
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:08,640
So get ready.

56
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:10,540
Strap in. And then he did really well.

57
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:11,660
All right.

58
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:13,500
So there are different reasons and yes,

59
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:16,000
so I think it's extremely likely

60
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,760
that if you kept a person sleep deprived for 24 hours before a

61
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:19,980
test, performance would be fine.

62
00:02:19,980 --> 00:02:22,080
Things like that.

63
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:25,460
Another complaint is that some super smart guy

64
00:02:25,460 --> 00:02:27,840
that you've heard of got a low score once.

65
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:30,440
So Richard Feynman supposedly got a low score

66
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:31,400
in an IQ test once.

67
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:34,400
I've heard this repeated many times as to what it means.

68
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:37,200
So if it's a test where there's a penalty for guessing,

69
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:38,920
then actually you'd have to be a genius

70
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:41,220
to get the minimum score, right?

71
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:44,800
So because, you know, it's a regular test

72
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:45,980
where you get marked out for guessing.

73
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:48,720
Now how could you get the wrong answer every time?

74
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:50,220
Only if you knew the right answer

75
00:02:50,220 --> 00:02:51,560
and deliberately did the opposite.

76
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:54,860
So it would take a very smart person to min

77
00:02:54,860 --> 00:02:56,940
out their score on a test like that.

78
00:02:56,940 --> 00:02:59,420
Of course, it doesn't take any intelligence at all

79
00:02:59,420 --> 00:03:00,620
to just leave the whole exam blank.

80
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:02,980
The real genius is getting a negative score.

81
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:06,620
Moderate negative score might not prove anything,

82
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:09,920
but if you get a really negative score, that's a challenge.

83
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:10,820
All right.

84
00:03:10,820 --> 00:03:11,980
So maybe that's what's going on.

85
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:13,320
All right.

86
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:17,780
Now once I was in the Johnson Center Library

87
00:03:17,780 --> 00:03:19,740
and I had like 20 minutes to kill,

88
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:21,660
and I just randomly picked up a book off the shelf called

89
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:24,860
The ETS's Rank of Error, which was an anti-testing,

90
00:03:24,860 --> 00:03:27,640
educational testing service book by Ralph Nader's organization.

91
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:30,200
And I clicked on a random page and it said,

92
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:33,060
do you know these tests perfectly predict GPA only 20%

93
00:03:33,060 --> 00:03:33,540
of the time?

94
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:37,860
I'm like, they perfectly predict GPA 20% of the time?

95
00:03:37,860 --> 00:03:38,780
That's incredible.

96
00:03:38,780 --> 00:03:41,740
I mean, like how many decimal places?

97
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:44,120
I mean, like it didn't say, but I was like, wow,

98
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:45,780
what an incredible endorsement of the test.

99
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:47,860
Way better than I would have thought.

100
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:50,900
But of course, the complaint was like 80%

101
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:52,260
of the time they give a wrong answer.

102
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:53,340
Well, how wrong?

103
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:54,660
This is statistics.

104
00:03:54,660 --> 00:03:57,380
It's not math where we expect the answer to be right

105
00:03:57,860 --> 00:03:59,820
to an infinite infinity of decimal places.

106
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:02,340
It's a real world problem where to be right

107
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:04,260
to even one decimal place is pretty good.

108
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:05,560
All right.

109
00:04:06,340 --> 00:04:07,980
So anyway, so all this is true.

110
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:10,300
Like all those, so many problems with IQ tests.

111
00:04:10,860 --> 00:04:12,400
I mean, it just goes on and on.

112
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:13,660
Actually, there's a giant literature.

113
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:15,860
Amazingly, people who are really interested

114
00:04:15,860 --> 00:04:18,500
in IQ spend a lot of time finding out all the problems

115
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:19,140
and publishing them.

116
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:21,460
Just like economists, really into markets.

117
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:23,860
We are the people who have all these complaints about markets.

118
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:25,620
We can make a career complaining about markets.

119
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:26,780
All right.

120
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:30,100
So anyway, important thing is that IQ tests are more reliable

121
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:32,060
than anything else, especially if you want

122
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:34,980
to predict someone's income or educational success

123
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:38,100
or their occupational attainment when they're seven years old.

124
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:40,240
And you only get one variable.

125
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:43,700
IQ is the best variable to get.

126
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:46,320
Does this mean that if you get a low score, you're doomed?

127
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:46,860
Of course not.

128
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:49,320
There's plenty of people who do badly

129
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:52,040
and they just are having a bad day or had some bad luck

130
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:53,380
and things still work out for them.

131
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:55,300
And of course, you can get a great score

132
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:56,500
and still become a total failure.

133
00:04:56,500 --> 00:04:57,620
It's also open to you.

134
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:01,600
But nevertheless, once you apply the normal standards

135
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:05,360
of statistics, which is don't expect too much, be grateful

136
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:08,200
for what we can predict, then IQ tests look

137
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:09,000
like they're looking pretty good.

138
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,200
So IQ tests are more reliable than anything else.

139
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:13,880
Very broadly useful.

140
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:16,860
So there's an enormous number of things

141
00:05:16,860 --> 00:05:17,840
that you can predict with IQ.

142
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:19,180
All right.

143
00:05:19,180 --> 00:05:21,200
And furthermore, if you test 100 geniuses,

144
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:22,540
their average score would be very high.

145
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:28,100
So there's a famous study called, it was done

146
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:29,860
by a psychologist named Terman.

147
00:05:29,860 --> 00:05:32,420
And then the people in the study were called the Termites.

148
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:33,660
All right.

149
00:05:33,660 --> 00:05:35,880
Anyway, so what happened was in the state of California

150
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:39,100
in I think the 20s, there was a massive statewide test.

151
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:42,160
And then he went and found 100 kids who scored highest

152
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:44,120
on the test and he studied them for the rest of their lives.

153
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:47,440
Now, funny thing, there was a Nobel Laureate named William

154
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:52,300
Shockley who was notorious for his very vocal,

155
00:05:52,300 --> 00:05:53,840
vocally pro IQ views.

156
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:58,580
And he was tested in this bunch and did not make the cut.

157
00:05:59,100 --> 00:06:02,340
So a Nobel Prize winner was not, did not make it

158
00:06:02,340 --> 00:06:03,340
into the Terman pool.

159
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:06,180
And then I don't think any of the other 100 ever won

160
00:06:06,180 --> 00:06:06,860
a Nobel Prize.

161
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:08,700
So stupid test, right?

162
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:11,420
Well, Terman was like a number 110 in the state.

163
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:14,580
So yes, he was not in the top 100, he was 110.

164
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:17,940
But still, he wasn't like number 5 million kid.

165
00:06:18,420 --> 00:06:19,980
He was still near the top.

166
00:06:19,980 --> 00:06:21,460
He just was a little bit worse

167
00:06:21,460 --> 00:06:22,820
than the very best one in the state.

168
00:06:23,060 --> 00:06:25,180
So not so shocking.

169
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:26,300
All right.

170
00:06:26,300 --> 00:06:27,820
So what can we say about intelligence?

171
00:06:27,820 --> 00:06:29,120
It's a lot like strength.

172
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:32,140
There's some ambiguity because it's a word that's existed

173
00:06:32,140 --> 00:06:34,260
for thousands of years, like how could we do without it?

174
00:06:34,260 --> 00:06:36,820
It's not going to have mathematical precision.

175
00:06:37,820 --> 00:06:39,820
Right? So there's some ambiguity,

176
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:41,080
but we do know what we mean.

177
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:43,960
If you call someone strong, you have an idea what that means.

178
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:46,060
And there's different observations you can have

179
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:48,320
where you would say, no, he's not, or he clearly is.

180
00:06:49,300 --> 00:06:51,140
Right? And there's ways that you would try to measure it,

181
00:06:51,140 --> 00:06:52,580
which would make sense to almost anyone

182
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:53,780
who understands the language.

183
00:06:54,100 --> 00:06:57,340
And since we know what we, so we basically know what we mean.

184
00:06:57,840 --> 00:06:58,940
We know there's real differences.

185
00:06:58,940 --> 00:07:00,340
So some people are stronger than me.

186
00:07:00,340 --> 00:07:01,860
Some people are smarter than me.

187
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:03,860
Right? That's just a fact.

188
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:07,380
Right? And also we know that people are strong

189
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:09,200
by one measure, usually strong by other measures too.

190
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:11,500
So someone can bench press a lot,

191
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:14,060
and they probably also can do a lot in a high squat.

192
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:15,540
They probably have a strong neck.

193
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:17,260
They probably even have strong fingers.

194
00:07:17,900 --> 00:07:19,480
Now if there were a football player who turned

195
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:21,500
out to have a really weak pinky or something like that,

196
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:25,380
would we then say, well sure, he's great on 99 of the tests

197
00:07:25,380 --> 00:07:27,460
of strength, but he has a below average pinky.

198
00:07:27,460 --> 00:07:30,820
Like would you then say he's weak?

199
00:07:30,820 --> 00:07:32,340
I would say no, no, he's a super strong guy

200
00:07:32,340 --> 00:07:33,500
with like a, with a weak pinky.

201
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:35,740
Yeah, but you know, like you, you know, you would go

202
00:07:35,740 --> 00:07:37,340
and say, all right, let's go and put a little bit of weight

203
00:07:37,340 --> 00:07:40,100
on that so we won't call him quite as strong as we would have

204
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:42,040
if he also had great pinky strength,

205
00:07:42,700 --> 00:07:44,700
but still he's a strong guy.

206
00:07:46,360 --> 00:07:47,860
Okay. All right.

207
00:07:47,860 --> 00:07:49,600
Now there's a second debate which we are not going

208
00:07:49,600 --> 00:07:52,620
to talk about other than to mention that it exists.

209
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:55,380
But that's the extent to which IQ is hereditary

210
00:07:55,380 --> 00:07:56,160
or environmental.

211
00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:58,080
You know, what is very well established is

212
00:07:58,080 --> 00:07:59,860
that IQ doesn't change much past a certain age.

213
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:03,460
You can, it is totally possible

214
00:08:03,460 --> 00:08:06,180
to raise someone's IQ score on a particular test.

215
00:08:06,980 --> 00:08:09,440
But, but this is a, this is a special case,

216
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:11,380
a more general rule, which is it's possible

217
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:13,400
to raise anybody's score on any test whatsoever.

218
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:16,180
How can you raise anybody's score

219
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:17,860
on anything, on any test whatsoever?

220
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:19,800
Teach to the test.

221
00:08:20,340 --> 00:08:21,100
Teach to the test.

222
00:08:21,220 --> 00:08:22,100
That's the simple rule.

223
00:08:22,860 --> 00:08:23,120
All right.

224
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:25,220
So that's the, so like you just want, but,

225
00:08:25,220 --> 00:08:27,360
but there is one problem with teaching the test,

226
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:29,940
which is if you then go and give them another test,

227
00:08:29,940 --> 00:08:31,520
which is supposed to test the same thing,

228
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:33,880
but it's not the same kind of test, then you don't see,

229
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:34,940
then you see very little gain.

230
00:08:35,980 --> 00:08:37,520
So if I go and I prep you for the SAT,

231
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:39,880
I can raise your SAT score, but that's not going to go

232
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:41,520
and make you do better on the Wexler test,

233
00:08:41,900 --> 00:08:45,300
or the Wunderlich test, or some other unrelated test.

234
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:47,320
All right.

235
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:50,140
So these are what researchers call hollow gains.

236
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:54,540
We have made you look better just by going and drilling you

237
00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:56,340
on the superficial aspects of the test.

238
00:08:57,060 --> 00:08:58,740
You know, which again is functionally not so different

239
00:08:58,740 --> 00:08:59,900
from us giving you the answers, right?

240
00:09:00,860 --> 00:09:02,640
If someone went and gave you the answer key for a test,

241
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:05,280
and you memorized it A, B, B, B, C, A, B, C, D,

242
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:07,720
and you got a perfect score on the test, are you now a genius?

243
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:09,840
No. It's like, well, why not?

244
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:11,340
So you got a genius score on the test.

245
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:12,340
Just doesn't that count?

246
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:14,360
How would you know it doesn't count?

247
00:09:14,360 --> 00:09:15,720
Well, because those, the gains,

248
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:18,600
once you've memorized the answer key, aren't going to lead

249
00:09:18,600 --> 00:09:21,320
to better performance on other tests that are supposed

250
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:23,020
to test the same thing, but where you didn't make it

251
00:09:23,020 --> 00:09:24,800
to memorize in advance.

252
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:27,000
So anyway, so there's no time to resolve the extent

253
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:28,760
to which IQ is registered environmental.

254
00:09:29,020 --> 00:09:34,040
We can say it's stable beyond a certain age, but if you go

255
00:09:34,040 --> 00:09:37,080
and read carefully constructed twin adoption studies,

256
00:09:37,520 --> 00:09:40,360
they will say that variance in IQ is about 80% genetic,

257
00:09:41,180 --> 00:09:43,860
and that about 20% is just noise, where it's very hard

258
00:09:43,860 --> 00:09:46,340
to figure out what's going on, and then almost none

259
00:09:46,340 --> 00:09:48,100
of it is explained by the family you're raised by,

260
00:09:48,700 --> 00:09:49,960
at least within rich countries.

261
00:09:50,560 --> 00:09:52,360
These data sets all come from first world countries,

262
